\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf1\f0\fs28\lang9 Lecture - ( Vectors in C++ )\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0 Vectors look same like as an array\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab} They are dynamic in nature mean size is not fixed\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab} STL is Standard Template Library in which some code of these datastructures is already implemented. Like :- stack , queue etc \par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab} From this library we pick these already implemented datastructure as a tool and then we use that in our code.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab} Vector is also a tool whose implementation is already done in STL.\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}All these datastructures are known as STL containers whose implementation is already done in STL\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab} To access vector values\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\tab for(auto v : vec) then print v\par
This will work as a foreach loop for a vector , Here above v will stored values not a index like for loop \par
\par
Vectors Functions :- These we use to perform some kind of operations\par
size , push_back , pop_back , front , back , at\par
\par
Static ( Compile Time ) Vs Dynamic Allocation :-\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Basically in this we see how vectors are implemented in a memory\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Static memory allocate at compile time like ( int arr[5] = \{\} ; )\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}During compile time our compiler checks sytaxtical , header files etc \par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Dynamic allocation during runtime like ( vector<int> vec )\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 There are two types of memory :-\par
1. Stack :- Static Allocation performs in stack\line 2. Heap :- Heap allocation performs in heap\par
In vector let suppose u have push a single element , but now again u wanna push one more element then at that time a capacity of vector will get double and previous one memory get deleted automatically\par
Vector contains two property one is size & another one is capacity\par
1. Size means counting total no of elements present inside it\par
2. Capacity means how many elements it can store at that particular moment\par
XOR :- ( 0^0 :- 0 , 1^1 :- 0  , 0^1 :- 1 , 1^0 :- 1 )\par
BruteForce Approach :- Means a solution which is not optimised\par
\par
\cf1 Lecture - ( Kadane's Algorithm ) \cf0\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Kadane's algorithm work on to calculate sum of two numbers\par

\pard\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 ( Small Positive ) + ( Big Positive ) :- Big Positive Number\par
( Small Negative ) + ( Big Positive ) :- Big Positive Number\par
( Big Negative ) + ( Small Positive ) :- Big Negative Number\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 So if we get sum become negative number , then according to kadanes algorithm reset it to 0\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Although it's a type of dynamic programming algorithm ( which means we convert big size problem into small size problem )\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Like first we solve small size problem and then with help of that we solve big size problem\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf1\par
Lecture :- ( Moore's Voting Algorithm )\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0 According to moore's voting algorithm if we start tracking frequency from starting then at the end there will be maximum frequency of majority element only\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}If we get same element then increase frequency otherwise decrease it , after decreasing frequency too there will maximum frequency of majority element only \par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\cf1 Lecture :- ( Time & Space Complexity )\cf0\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Time complexity does not means the actual time taken , but it means the amount of time taken as a function of input size n .\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Amount of time taken means :- no of operations\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}So in actual TC means the no of operations as a function of input size n that's Time Complexity .\line\line Means as size of n increase , so at that time how many no of operations are getting perform .\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Basically TC tells the behviour how our algorithm is going to perform\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1      O(n) :- Linear Time Complexity\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 We always try to calculate TC in worst case scenario , because if our algo performs great then automatically it works on small input\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
Big O Notation :- \par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 It's a kind of symbol\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}It's shows a function of TC , not just a normal function .\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}It give complexity of worst case scenario and also known as upper bound\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Let suppose TC is O(n) which means in worst case scenario means isse jyada bura case ho hi nahin sakta \par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Example :- f(n) = 4n^2 + 3n + 2 \par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 First ignore constants\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Then consider only largest term\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1     \tab  TC :- O(n^2)\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Big Ohm :- Worst Case\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Omega :- Best Case\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Theta :- Average Case\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
Space Complexity :- Not an actual space but It's amount of space taken by an algorithm as a function of input size n .\par

\pard{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf1\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Our code contains two type of spaces Actual input & Auxilary Space ( Extra Space )\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}To calculate SC , we only count extra space not actual space\par
{\pntext\f1\'B7\tab}Size of variable will always be same or constant does not matter wheather size of array if vector is getting decrease or increase\par

